WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.250
Build it, break it on purpose, then hand it over.

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.910
This is the video for module two of Automation with Zapier and Make.

3
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:16.290
In this module you build a real Zap. Every time someone fills a Google Form, they get a confirmation email.

4
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:21.420
Then you build a Make scenario that sends each order down the right route.

5
00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:27.580
You test it with messy data, because one clean test row does not mean it is finished.

6
00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.580
And you leave a run book, so the client can keep it running without you.

7
00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:44.710
Create a form with Full name, Email, Phone, Item ordered and Quantity. In the Responses tab, link it to a new Google Sheet. Every response becomes a new row.

8
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:51.950
In Zapier, create a Zap. The trigger is Google Sheets, New Spreadsheet Row, on the response sheet.

9
00:00:53.200 --> 00:01:00.120
Submit one test entry yourself, then test the trigger, so Zapier pulls in a real row with all its fields.

10
00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:10.710
The action is Gmail, Send Email. Map the To box to the Email field. Use Full name, Item ordered and Quantity in the subject and body.

11
00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:16.440
Test the action, read the email as a customer would, then publish the Zap.

12
00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:29.870
Keep the trigger sheet clean. The Zap finds fields by the header row, so never rename or reorder it, and never type notes or totals into that sheet. Do calculations on a second tab.

13
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:41.700
For example, Grace sells bead bags in Port Harcourt and expects about 60 orders a month. Her email says: thank you for ordering 2 times Mini beaded clutch.

14
00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:48.670
Her Zap has one action, so each order uses one task. That is 60 tasks a month.

15
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.480
That fits inside the free 100 tasks.

16
00:01:53.730 --> 00:02:01.070
Her sister tests from her own phone. The email arrives within about 15 minutes, as expected for a polling trigger.

17
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:15.780
A safety point about money. Fraudsters copy business names and send fake account numbers. So keep payment details in one place the owner controls, and check every automated message that mentions money.

18
00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:21.250
Grace's email says payment details will come on WhatsApp from her known business number.

19
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.110
Never let customer-typed text decide where money is paid.

20
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:38.000
For example, Kemi sells hair products from Lagos. In Make, the trigger watches her order sheet for new rows. During a trial, a check every 2 hours is sensible.

21
00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:44.820
Next comes a router. It uses no credits and lets each order travel down different routes.

22
00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:52.150
On the first route, a filter lets through State equal to Lagos, and Gmail emails the rider.

23
00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:59.200
On the second, State is not Lagos, so a row is added to the Interstate tab for the courier.

24
00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:10.310
What if State is blank, or typed with an extra space? Trim spaces, match without caring about capital letters, and add a fallback route that alerts the owner.

25
00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:21.160
Test on purpose with bad data. Try blank fields, phone numbers typed different ways, names with apostrophes, and a customer who submits twice.

26
00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:33.540
Test a failure too. Make, by default, switches off a scheduled scenario after 3 errors in a row. So send error alerts to you and to a named person at the client.

27
00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:48.360
For example, Chidinma's enrolment scenario stopped when the admin changed a Google password. Musa saw the alert that morning. She reconnected, retried the stored runs, and all 9 waiting enrolments went through.

28
00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:58.190
Tick every box before you hand over. The normal case passes. Edge cases are handled or flagged. Failure alerts reach a person.

29
00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:10.800
Personal data is kept to what each step needs. Nigeria has the Nigeria Data Protection Act 2023, so build in the client's own accounts and remove your access at the end.

30
00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:13.050
And the run book is written and shared.

31
00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:30.580
To remember. Keep the trigger sheet clean. Test with a real submission. Give every router a fallback. Send error alerts to a real person. And write a one-page run book: what it does, which accounts it uses, how to tell it works, and who to call.

32
00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:42.560
Now build your first Zap and test it from two devices. Build a two-route scenario with a fallback. Then break your own automation with a test plan, and write the run book.

33
00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:44.850
Continue with the lessons below.
